<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">O Come, All Ye Faithful</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first printed <a href="/years/1751">1751</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Adeste Fideles</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;O Come, All Ye Faithful&quot;, first known by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> title &quot;Adeste Fideles&quot;, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> of uncertain origin. Its words have been credited at various times to St. Bonaventure in the 13th century, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> IV of Portugal in the 17th, and most often to Cistercian monks of the German, Portuguese or Spanish provinces of the order.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest printed version appears in a collection by John Francis Wade, an English Catholic who lived in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and made a living copying musical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. In 1751 he published Cantus Diversi pro Dominicis et Festis per annum, the first printed source for Adeste Fideles, and a manuscript of 1751 by Wade is held at Stonyhurst College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>. His name is on the earliest printed versions, and modern English hymnals usually credit the text to him. Musicologists agree that it was through Wade that the hymn first appeared in print, but its exact sources remain unproven.</p><p>A parody of the chorus was current before 1542, when it appears in Rabelais' Gargantua. The tune has been attributed to Wade, to John Reading and his son, to Handel, to Gluck, to Marcos Portugal, to King John IV of Portugal and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-arne">Thomas Arne</a>, whom Wade knew.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Wade's version had four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> verses, set in the traditional square notation of medieval liturgical music and in triple meter, unlike modern versions. In the later 18th century the French priest Jean-François-Étienne Borderies wrote three further Latin verses, and another anonymous verse is rarely printed. Most of the original lyrics are an almost verbatim expression of Catholic doctrine about Jesus Christ, taking nearly all their content from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> verses and the Nicene Creed.</p><p>Bennett Zon, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-university">Durham University</a>, has proposed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> text was a coded call to the Jacobite cause: that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a> stood for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and that the phrase Regem Angelorum, king of angels, punned on Anglorum, of the English, naming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Charles Edward Stuart</a>. Wade fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after the failure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1745">Jacobite rising of 1745</a>, and his books carried Jacobite imagery. The claims are unproven, and no text shows that Wade wrote the hymn as propaganda.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1841 the English Catholic priest Frederick Oakeley translated the hymn into English; he first titled it Ye Faithful, approach ye, for his Margaret Chapel in Marylebone. The common version combines one of Oakeley's translations of the original four verses with William Thomas Brooke's translation of the three added verses, and was first published in Murray's Hymnal in 1852. The hymn was sometimes called the Portuguese Hymn, after the Duke of Leeds heard it at the Portuguese embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1795. A German translation appeared in 1823, and it has been translated into many languages. Adeste Fideles is traditionally the final anthem at Midnight Mass in St Peter's Basilica in the Vatican.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “O Come, All Ye Faithful” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
